@node	2.3, 2.4, 2.2, Chapter 2
@section Symbolic Data

All the compound data objects we have used so far were constructed ultimately
from numbers.  In this section we extend the representational capability of our
language by introducing the ability to work with arbitrary symbols as data.

@menu
* 2.3.1::            Quotation
* 2.3.2::            Example: Symbolic Differentiation
* 2.3.3::            Example: Representing Sets
* 2.3.4::            Example: Huffman Encoding Trees
@end menu

@node	2.3.1, 2.3.2, 2.3, 2.3
@subsection Quotation

If we can form compound data using symbols, we can have lists such as

@example ocaml
[ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c"; Sym "d" ]
[ 23; 45; 17 ]
[ ("Norah", 12)
; ("Molly", 9)
; ("Anna", 7)
; ("Lauren", 6)
; ("Charlotte", 4)
]
@end example

@noindent
A symbol is the constructor @code{Sym} wrapped around a name, one small
variant that gives OCaml a value symbols can occupy without disturbing the
type of anything else.@footnote{The name inside @code{Sym} is an ordinary
double-quoted string, the same syntax OCaml uses for every other string in
the language.  Scheme instead marks a symbol with a leading single quote and
reserves the double quote for character strings, so the two quote marks must
stay visually distinct from each other.  OCaml has only the one quote mark,
because @code{Sym} itself, not a second kind of punctuation, is what marks a
string as a symbol rather than ordinary text.}  Lists containing symbols
look nothing like the expressions of our language, because in OCaml the two
are different grammars, never confused:

@example ocaml
let rec fact n = if n = 1 then 1 else n * fact (n - 1);;

fact 5;;
(* => 120 *)
@end example

@noindent
In Scheme, the corresponding list @code{(* (+ 23 45) (+ x 9))} could equally
well be typed at the interpreter as a program that multiplies two sums, or
handed to @code{'quote} as inert data spelling out a multiplication.  A Scheme
reader must be told, at the moment it reads the parentheses, which one it is
building.  An OCaml compiler never faces that choice: a data literal such as
@code{[ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c"; Sym "d" ]} is built once, when the program
runs, by calling the @code{Sym} constructor three times and consing the
results into a list; nothing about that act can also be read back as an
instruction to look up a variable named @code{a} or to apply a
function.@footnote{Scheme's own @code{quote} special form makes the same
point from the other side: @code{'a} is only an abbreviation the reader
expands into @code{(quote a)}, so @code{(car '(a b c))} and
@code{(car (quote (a b c)))} are the identical program.  A quoted list is
still built by evaluating an ordinary call, @code{quote}, whose result
happens to be its own unevaluated argument.  OCaml's @code{[ Sym "a"; Sym
"b"; Sym "c" ]} is built by an ordinary call too, to the list constructor
and to @code{Sym}, with no abbreviation and no special form standing in for
either.}  In
order to manipulate symbols, Scheme needs a new element in the language: the
ability to @newterm{quote} a data object so that the reader stops short of
treating it as a call.  OCaml needs no such device, because @newterm{values}
and the names that denote them already live on opposite sides of the
compiler.@footnote{Allowing quotation in a language wreaks havoc with the
ability to reason about the language in simple terms, because it destroys the
notion that equals can be substituted for equals.  For example, three is one
plus two, but the word ``three'' is not the phrase ``one plus two.''
Quotation is powerful because it gives us a way to build expressions that
manipulate other expressions (as we will see when we write an interpreter in
@ref{Chapter 4}).  But allowing statements in a language that talk about
other statements in that language makes it very difficult to maintain any
coherent principle of what ``equals can be substituted for equals'' should
mean.  For example, if we know that the evening star is the morning star,
then from the statement ``the evening star is Venus'' we can deduce ``the
morning star is Venus.''  However, given that ``John knows that the evening
star is Venus'' we cannot infer that ``John knows that the morning star is
Venus.''}

Now we can distinguish between symbols and the values of the names that
denote them:

@example ocaml
let a = 1;;
let b = 2;;

[ a; b ];;
(* => [1; 2] *)

[ Sym "a"; Sym "b" ];;
(* => [Sym "a"; Sym "b"] *)

( Sym "a", b );;
(* => (Sym "a", 2) *)
@end example

@noindent
The third line is a pair, not a list: @code{Sym "a"} is a @code{symbol} and
@code{b}'s value is an @code{int}, and OCaml's lists hold elements of one type
only.  Scheme's @code{(list 'a b)} can mix a symbol and a number in one list
because a Scheme list carries no type at all; the moment two kinds of data
need to sit side by side in an OCaml list, some single type has to be found or
built that both of them fit, the way a pair fits them here.  We will meet the
same choice again, built into a proper variant, in @ref{Exercise 2.54}.

@example ocaml
List.hd [ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c" ];;
(* => Sym "a" *)

List.tl [ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c" ];;
(* => [Sym "b"; Sym "c"] *)
@end example

@noindent
The empty list @code{[]} plays the part of Scheme's quoted empty list
@code{'()}; OCaml needs no quote mark to write it, any more than it needs one
to write @code{[ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c" ]}.

One additional operation used in manipulating symbols is structural equality,
@code{=}, which OCaml already gives every type built without functions inside
it, @code{symbol} among them.@footnote{We can consider two symbols to be
``the same'' if they consist of the same characters in the same order.  Such a
definition skirts a deep issue that we are not yet ready to address: the
meaning of ``sameness'' in a programming language.  We will return to this in
@ref{Chapter 3} (@ref{3.1.3}).}  Using @code{=}, we can implement a useful
procedure called @code{memq}.  This takes two arguments, an item and a list.
If the item is not contained in the list (i.e., is not @code{=} to any item in
the list), then @code{memq} returns @code{None}.  Otherwise, it returns
@code{Some} the sublist of the list beginning with the first occurrence of the
item:

@example ocaml
let rec memq item = function
  | [] -> None
  | first :: rest ->
    if first = item then Some (first :: rest) else memq item rest
;;
@end example

@noindent
For example, the value of

@example ocaml
memq (Sym "apple") [ Sym "pear"; Sym "banana"; Sym "prune" ]
@end example

@noindent
is @code{None}, whereas the value of

@example ocaml
memq (Sym "apple") [ Sym "x"; Sym "y"; Sym "apple"; Sym "pear" ]
@end example

@noindent
is @code{Some [Sym "apple"; Sym "pear"]}.  In Scheme, a symbol can hide one
level down, inside a sublist: searching @code{(memq 'red '((red shoes) (blue
socks)))} finds nothing at the top level, because the first element is the
whole list @code{(red shoes)}, not the symbol @code{red}.  A @code{symbol
list} cannot hide a symbol that way: every element of the list is a
@code{symbol}, in full view of @code{memq}, so the question ``is @code{red}
one of the elements, or only part of one of them'' has already been answered
by the type before @code{memq} is ever called.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.53}Exercise 2.53:} @emph{SICP exercise 2.53 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps
the number and teaches the same section material.}  Predict what OCaml
prints in response to evaluating each of the following expressions.

@example ocaml
[ Sym "a"; Sym "b"; Sym "c" ]
[ [ Sym "george" ] ]
List.tl [ [ Sym "x1"; Sym "x2" ]; [ Sym "y1"; Sym "y2" ] ]
List.hd (List.tl [ [ Sym "x1"; Sym "x2" ]; [ Sym "y1"; Sym "y2" ] ])
List.hd [ Sym "a"; Sym "short"; Sym "list" ] = Sym "a"
memq (Sym "red") [ Sym "shoes"; Sym "blue"; Sym "socks" ]
memq (Sym "red") [ Sym "red"; Sym "shoes"; Sym "blue"; Sym "socks" ]
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.54}Exercise 2.54:} Two data values are said to be
@code{equal_datum} if they contain equal elements arranged in the same order.
Because a Scheme list can freely mix symbols and sublists but an OCaml list
cannot, state this over the small variant @code{type datum = Sym of string |
Seq of datum list}, the general shape that a Scheme quoted list actually has.
For example,

@example ocaml
equal_datum
  (Seq [ Sym "this"; Sym "is"; Sym "a"; Sym "list" ])
  (Seq [ Sym "this"; Sym "is"; Sym "a"; Sym "list" ])
@end example

@noindent
is @code{true}, but

@example ocaml
equal_datum
  (Seq [ Sym "this"; Sym "is"; Sym "a"; Sym "list" ])
  (Seq [ Sym "this"; Seq [ Sym "is"; Sym "a" ]; Sym "list" ])
@end example

@noindent
is @code{false}.  To be more precise, we can define @code{equal_datum}
recursively in terms of the equality of symbols by saying that @code{a} and
@code{b} are @code{equal_datum} if they are both symbols and the symbols are
equal, or if they are both sequences of the same length whose elements are
pairwise @code{equal_datum}.  Using this idea, implement @code{equal_datum} as
a procedure.@footnote{In practice, programmers want to compare data that mixes
numbers with symbols, which would mean adding a third constructor,
@code{Num of int}, to @code{datum}.  Scheme needs a footnote here because
@code{eq?} is a pointer test, and whether two numerically equal numbers
(as tested by @code{=}) are also @code{eq?} is implementation-dependent; a
correct @code{equal?} has to fall back to @code{=} for numbers rather than
@code{eq?}.  OCaml's structural @code{=} already does the right thing for
@code{int}, @code{string}, and any variant built from them, so
@code{equal_datum} would need no special case for a @code{Num} arm beyond the
one @code{=} already gives it.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.55}Exercise 2.55:} @emph{SICP exercise 2.55 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps
the number and teaches the same section material.}  Eva Lu Ator's puzzle
becomes a reading exercise in this edition's own terms.  The shared Scheme
subset of @ref{Chapter 4} has a real reader, @code{Sicp_common.Reader}, which
turns the abbreviation @code{'x} into a @code{Quote} node wrapping the datum
@code{(quote x)}; reading @code{''abracadabra} therefore quotes the
two-element list @code{(quote abracadabra)}.  Using @code{Reader.read} and
@code{Ast.view}, extract the datum a top-level quote wraps, take its @code{car},
and read off the symbol's name.  Explain why the answer is @code{quote}.
@end quotation

@node	2.3.2, 2.3.3, 2.3.1, 2.3
@subsection Example: Symbolic Differentiation

As an illustration of symbol manipulation and a further illustration of data
abstraction, consider the design of a procedure that performs symbolic
differentiation of algebraic expressions.  We would like the procedure to take
as arguments an algebraic expression and a variable and to return the
derivative of the expression with respect to the variable.  For example, if the
arguments to the procedure are @math{{ax^2 + bx + c}} and @math{x}, the
procedure should return @math{{2ax + b}}.  Symbolic differentiation is of
special historical significance in Lisp.  It was one of the motivating examples
behind the development of a computer language for symbol manipulation.
Furthermore, it marked the beginning of the line of research that led to the
development of powerful systems for symbolic mathematical work, which are
currently being used by a growing number of applied mathematicians and
physicists.

In developing the symbolic-differentiation program, we will follow the same
strategy of data abstraction that we followed in developing the rational-number
system of @ref{2.1.1}.  That is, we will first define a differentiation
algorithm that operates on abstract objects such as ``sums,'' ``products,'' and
``variables'' without worrying about how these are to be represented.  Only
afterward will we address the representation problem.

@subsubheading The differentiation program with abstract data

In order to keep things simple, we will consider a very simple
symbolic-differentiation program that handles expressions that are built up
using only the operations of addition and multiplication with two arguments.
Differentiation of any such expression can be carried out by applying the
following reduction rules:
@displaymath
{{dc \over dx} \,=\, 0,}
  \quad {\hbox{for } c \hbox{ a constant }} {\hbox{or a variable }} {\hbox{different from } x,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dc
-- = 0  for c a constant, or a variable different from x
dx

dx
-- = 1
dx

d(u + v)   du   dv
-------- = -- + --
   dx      dx   dx

d(uv)     / dv \     / du \
----- = u | -- | + v | -- |
 dx       \ dx /     \ dx /
@end example

@end ifinfo
@displaymath
\eqalign{
{dx \over dx}         &=   1, \cr
{d(u + v) \over dx}   &=   {du \over dx} + {dv \over dx}, \cr
{d(uv) \over dx}      &=   u \kern0.1em {dv \over dx} + v \kern0.1em {du \over dx}.
}
@end displaymath
@noindent
Observe that the latter two rules are recursive in nature.  That is, to obtain
the derivative of a sum we first find the derivatives of the terms and add
them.  Each of the terms may in turn be an expression that needs to be
decomposed.  Decomposing into smaller and smaller pieces will eventually
produce pieces that are either constants or variables, whose derivatives will
be either 0 or 1.

To embody these rules in a procedure we indulge in a little wishful thinking,
as we did in designing the rational-number implementation.  If we had a means
for representing algebraic expressions, we should be able to tell whether an
expression is a sum, a product, a constant, or a variable.  In Scheme those
four shapes are told apart at run time, by inspecting whatever list structure
happens to be sitting in the argument; in OCaml we declare the four shapes as
one @newterm{variant} up front, and the four ``predicates'' the book asks for
become the four constructors of that variant, checked once and for all by the
compiler at every place a value of the type is built or matched:

@example ocaml
type expr =
  | Const of int
  | Var of string
  | Sum of expr * expr
  | Prod of expr * expr
@end example

@noindent
We should be able to extract the parts of an expression.  For a sum, for
example we want to be able to extract the addend (first term) and the augend
(second term).  We should also be able to construct expressions from parts.
Let us assume that we already have procedures to implement the following
selectors, constructors, and predicates:

@example
is_variable e          (* Is e a variable? *)
same_variable v1 v2    (* Are v1 and v2 the same variable? *)
is_sum e               (* Is e a sum? *)
addend e               (* Addend of the sum e. *)
augend e               (* Augend of the sum e. *)
make_sum a1 a2         (* Construct the sum of a1 and a2. *)
is_product e           (* Is e a product? *)
multiplier e           (* Multiplier of the product e. *)
multiplicand e         (* Multiplicand of the product e. *)
make_product m1 m2     (* Construct the product of m1 and m2. *)
@end example

@noindent
Using these, we can express the differentiation rules as the following
procedure:

@example ocaml
let rec deriv exp var =
  match exp with
  | Const _ -> Const 0
  | Var _ -> if same_variable exp (Var var) then Const 1 else Const 0
  | Sum (a1, a2) -> make_sum (deriv a1 var) (deriv a2 var)
  | Prod (m1, m2) ->
    make_sum
      (make_product m1 (deriv m2 var))
      (make_product (deriv m1 var) m2)
;;
@end example

@noindent
This @code{deriv} procedure incorporates the complete differentiation
algorithm.  Because @code{exp}'s type names every shape the compiler will let
through, the four-way @code{match} above needs no final ``else, signal an
unknown-expression error'' clause: a value that is a @code{Const}, a
@code{Var}, a @code{Sum}, or a @code{Prod} is the only kind of value
@code{expr} can hold, so every case really is covered.  It will work no
matter how we choose to represent algebraic expressions, as long as we design
a proper set of selectors and constructors.  This is the issue we must
address next.

@subsubheading Representing algebraic expressions

We can imagine many ways to represent algebraic expressions with the
@code{expr} variant above.  The representation we chose stores a sum or a
product's two operands directly inside the constructor, instead of, say,
naming them with a Scheme-style symbol at the head of a list; that is, we
represent @math{{ax + b}} directly as @code{Sum (Prod (Var "a", Var "x"), Var
"b")}.  Then our data representation for the differentiation problem is as
follows:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The variables carry their name as a string, wrapped in the @code{Var}
constructor.  A value is a variable when it matches @code{Var _}:

@example ocaml
let is_variable = function Var _ -> true | _ -> false
@end example

@item
Two variables are the same if the names inside them are equal:

@example ocaml
let same_variable v1 v2 =
  is_variable v1 && is_variable v2 && v1 = v2
;;
@end example

@item
Sums and products are constructed with @code{Sum} and @code{Prod}:

@example ocaml
let make_sum a1 a2 = Sum (a1, a2)
let make_product m1 m2 = Prod (m1, m2)
@end example

@item
A value is a sum when it matches @code{Sum _}, and a product when it matches
@code{Prod _}:

@example ocaml
let is_sum = function Sum _ -> true | _ -> false
let is_product = function Prod _ -> true | _ -> false
@end example

@item
The addend and the augend are the first and second parts of a @code{Sum};
the multiplier and the multiplicand are the first and second parts of a
@code{Prod}:

@example ocaml
let addend = function Sum (a, _) -> a | _ -> invalid_arg "addend: not a sum"
let augend = function Sum (_, a) -> a | _ -> invalid_arg "augend: not a sum"
let multiplier =
  function Prod (m, _) -> m | _ -> invalid_arg "multiplier: not a product"
let multiplicand =
  function Prod (_, m) -> m | _ -> invalid_arg "multiplicand: not a product"
@end example

@end itemize

@noindent
Thus, we need only combine these with the algorithm as embodied by
@code{deriv} to have a working symbolic-differentiation program.
Let us look at some examples of its behavior:

@example ocaml
deriv (Sum (Var "x", Const 3)) "x";;
(* => Sum (Const 1, Const 0) *)

deriv (Prod (Var "x", Var "y")) "x";;
(* => Sum (Prod (Var "x", Const 0), Prod (Const 1, Var "y")) *)

deriv (Prod (Prod (Var "x", Var "y"), Sum (Var "x", Const 3))) "x";;
(* => Sum (Prod (Prod (Var "x", Var "y"), Sum (Const 1, Const 0)),
          Prod (Sum (Prod (Var "x", Const 0), Prod (Const 1, Var "y")),
                Sum (Var "x", Const 3))) *)
@end example

@noindent
The program produces answers that are correct; however, they are unsimplified.
It is true that
@displaymath
{d(xy) \over dx} \,=\, {x \cdot 0} + {1 \cdot y,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(xy)
----- = x * 0 + 1 * y
 dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
but we would like the program to know that @math{{x \cdot 0 = 0}}, @math{{1 \cdot y = y}},
and @math{{0 + y = y}}.  The answer for the second example should have been
simply @code{y}.  As the third example shows, this becomes a serious issue when
the expressions are complex.

Our difficulty is much like the one we encountered with the rational-number
implementation: we haven't reduced answers to simplest form.  To accomplish the
rational-number reduction, we needed to change only the constructors and the
selectors of the implementation.  We can adopt a similar strategy here: we
won't change @code{deriv} at all, only @code{make_sum} and @code{make_product}.
In Scheme this revision simply rebinds the old @code{make-sum} and
@code{make-product} to new definitions; an OCaml source file cannot rebind a
name already defined above it, so the revised procedures below get their own
names, @code{Deriv.make_sum} and @code{Deriv.make_product} living in a second
module alongside the first, @code{Deriv_naive}, which keeps the unsimplified
version runnable.  @code{make_sum} will add two numbers together when both
summands are numbers, and if one of the summands is 0, @code{make_sum} will
return the other summand:

@example ocaml
let is_number exp n = match exp with Const m -> m = n | _ -> false

let make_sum a1 a2 =
  if is_number a1 0 then a2
  else if is_number a2 0 then a1
  else (
    match (a1, a2) with
    | Const m, Const n -> Const (m + n)
    | _ -> Sum (a1, a2))
;;
@end example

@noindent
This uses @code{is_number}, which checks whether an expression is a given
constant.  Similarly, @code{make_product} builds in the rules that 0 times
anything is 0 and 1 times anything is the thing itself:

@example ocaml
let make_product m1 m2 =
  if is_number m1 0 || is_number m2 0 then Const 0
  else if is_number m1 1 then m2
  else if is_number m2 1 then m1
  else (
    match (m1, m2) with
    | Const m, Const n -> Const (m * n)
    | _ -> Prod (m1, m2))
;;
@end example

@noindent
Here is how this version works on our three examples:

@example ocaml
deriv (Sum (Var "x", Const 3)) "x";;
(* => Const 1 *)

deriv (Prod (Var "x", Var "y")) "x";;
(* => Var "y" *)

deriv (Prod (Prod (Var "x", Var "y"), Sum (Var "x", Const 3))) "x";;
(* => Sum (Prod (Var "x", Var "y"), Prod (Var "y", Sum (Var "x", Const 3))) *)
@end example

@noindent
Although this is quite an improvement, the third example shows that there is
still a long way to go before we get a program that puts expressions into a
form that we might agree is ``simplest.''  The problem of algebraic
simplification is complex because, among other reasons, a form that may be
simplest for one purpose may not be for another.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.56}Exercise 2.56:} Show how to extend the basic
differentiator to handle more kinds of expressions.  For instance, implement
the differentiation rule
@displaymath
{d(u^{\kern0.1ex n}) \over dx} \,=\, {nu^{\kern0.1ex n-1} \, {du \over dx}}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(u^n)            du
------ = nu^(n-1) --
  dx              dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
by adding a new case to the @code{expr} variant, @code{Power of expr * expr},
and defining appropriate procedures @code{base}, @code{exponent}, and
@code{make_exponentiation}.  Build in the rules that anything raised to the
power 0 is 1 and anything raised to the power 1 is the thing itself.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.57}Exercise 2.57:} Extend the differentiation
program to handle sums and products of arbitrary numbers of (two or more)
terms, by changing @code{Sum} and @code{Prod} to hold an @code{expr list}
instead of a pair of expressions.  Then the last example above could be
expressed as

@example ocaml
deriv (Prod [ Var "x"; Var "y"; Sum [ Var "x"; Const 3 ] ]) "x"
@end example

Try to do this by changing only the representation for sums and products,
without changing the @code{deriv} procedure's shape at all.  For example, the
@code{addend} of a sum would be the first term, and the @code{augend} would be
the sum of the rest of the terms.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.58}Exercise 2.58:} Suppose we want to modify the
differentiation program so that it works with ordinary mathematical notation,
in which @code{+} and @code{*} are infix rather than prefix operators.  Since
the differentiation program is defined in terms of abstract data, we can modify
it to work with different representations of expressions solely by changing the
predicates, selectors, and constructors that define the representation of the
algebraic expressions on which the differentiator is to operate.

@enumerate a

@item
Show how to do this to differentiate algebraic expressions presented
in infix form, such as @code{x + (3 * (x + (y + 2)))}, represented as a new
variant @code{infix} with constructors @code{Num}, @code{Var}, @code{Plus},
and @code{Times}.  To simplify the task, assume that @code{+} and @code{*}
always take two arguments and that expressions are fully parenthesized.

@item
The problem becomes substantially harder if we allow standard algebraic
notation, such as @code{x + 3 * (x + y + 2)}, which drops unnecessary
parentheses and assumes that multiplication is done before addition.  Design a
tokenizer type and a @code{parse_standard} function that read such notation
into the very same @code{infix} tree of part (a), so that @code{deriv} needs
no second version.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@node	2.3.3, 2.3.4, 2.3.2, 2.3
@subsection Example: Representing Sets

In the previous examples we built representations for two kinds of compound
data objects: rational numbers and algebraic expressions.  In one of these
examples we had the choice of simplifying (reducing) the expressions at either
construction time or selection time, but other than that the choice of a
representation for these structures in terms of lists was straightforward.  When
we turn to the representation of sets, the choice of a representation is not so
obvious.  Indeed, there are a number of possible representations, and they
differ significantly from one another in several ways.

Informally, a set is simply a collection of distinct objects.  To give a more
precise definition we can employ the method of data abstraction.  That is, we
define ``set'' by specifying the operations that are to be used on sets.  These
are @code{union_set}, @code{intersection_set}, @code{element_of_set}, and
@code{adjoin_set}, gathered into one module @newterm{signature}:

@example ocaml
module type Set = sig
  type t

  val element_of_set : int -> t -> bool
  val adjoin_set : int -> t -> t
  val union_set : t -> t -> t
  val intersection_set : t -> t -> t
end
@end example

@noindent
@code{element_of_set} is a predicate that determines whether a given element
is a member of a set.  @code{adjoin_set} takes an object and a set as
arguments and returns a set that contains the elements of the original set and
also the adjoined element.  @code{union_set} computes the union of two sets,
which is the set containing each element that appears in either argument.
@code{intersection_set} computes the intersection of two sets, which is the
set containing only elements that appear in both arguments.  From the
viewpoint of data abstraction, we are free to design any module that
implements this signature in a way consistent with the interpretations given
above.@footnote{If we want to be more formal, we can specify ``consistent
with the interpretations given above'' to mean that the operations satisfy a
collection of rules such as these:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For any set @code{s} and any object @code{x},
@code{element_of_set x (adjoin_set x s)}
is @code{true} (informally: ``Adjoining an object to a
set produces a set that contains the object'').

@item
For any sets @code{s} and @code{t} and any object @code{x},
@code{element_of_set x (union_set s t)}
is equal to
@code{element_of_set x s || element_of_set x t}
(informally: ``The elements of @code{union_set s t} are the elements that
are in @code{s} or in @code{t}'').

@item
For any object @code{x},
@code{element_of_set x []}
is @code{false} (informally: ``No object is an element of the empty set'').

@end itemize
}

Below, each representation is given only the operations the running text has
developed for it; a module earns the @code{Set} signature above only once
Exercises 2.59 through 2.65 have supplied the operations still missing.

@subsubheading Sets as unordered lists

One way to represent a set is as a list of its elements in which no element
appears more than once.  The empty set is represented by the empty list.  In
this representation, @code{element_of_set} is similar to the procedure
@code{memq} of @ref{2.3.1}, except that it uses @code{=} directly (element
lists here hold plain @code{int}s rather than @code{symbol}s):

@example ocaml
let rec element_of_set x = function
  | [] -> false
  | first :: rest -> first = x || element_of_set x rest
;;
@end example

@noindent
Using this, we can write @code{adjoin_set}.  If the object to be adjoined is
already in the set, we just return the set.  Otherwise, we use @code{::} to
add the object to the list that represents the set:

@example ocaml
let adjoin_set x set = if element_of_set x set then set else x :: set
@end example

@noindent
For @code{intersection_set} we can use a recursive strategy.  If we know how to
form the intersection of @code{set2} and the rest of @code{set1}, we only
need to decide whether to include the first element of @code{set1} in this.
But this depends on whether that first element is also in @code{set2}.  Here is
the resulting procedure:

@example ocaml
let rec intersection_set set1 set2 =
  match set1 with
  | [] -> []
  | first :: rest ->
    if element_of_set first set2
    then first :: intersection_set rest set2
    else intersection_set rest set2
;;
@end example

@noindent
In designing a representation, one of the issues we should be concerned with is
efficiency.  Consider the number of steps required by our set operations.
Since they all use @code{element_of_set}, the speed of this operation has a
major impact on the efficiency of the set implementation as a whole.  Now, in
order to check whether an object is a member of a set, @code{element_of_set}
may have to scan the entire set.  (In the worst case, the object turns out not
to be in the set.)  Hence, if the set has @math{n} elements,
@code{element_of_set} might take up to @math{n} steps.  Thus, the number of
steps required grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.  The number of steps required by
@code{adjoin_set}, which uses this operation, also grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.
For @code{intersection_set}, which does an @code{element_of_set} check for
each element of @code{set1}, the number of steps required grows as the product
of the sizes of the sets involved, or @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} for two sets of size
@math{n}.  The same will be true of @code{union_set}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.59}Exercise 2.59:} Implement the @code{union_set}
operation for the unordered-list representation of sets.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.60}Exercise 2.60:} We specified that a set would be
represented as a list with no duplicates.  Now suppose we allow duplicates.
For instance, the set @math{{\{1, 2, 3\}}} could be represented as the list
@code{[2; 3; 2; 1; 3; 2; 2]}.  Design procedures @code{element_of_set},
@code{adjoin_set}, @code{union_set}, and @code{intersection_set} that operate
on this representation.  How does the efficiency of each compare with the
corresponding procedure for the non-duplicate representation?  Are there
applications for which you would use this representation in preference to the
non-duplicate one?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sets as ordered lists

One way to speed up our set operations is to change the representation so that
the set elements are listed in increasing order.  To keep our discussion
simple, we will consider only the case where the set elements are numbers, so
that we can compare elements using @code{>} and @code{<}.  We will represent a
set of numbers by listing its elements in increasing order.  Whereas our first
representation above allowed us to represent the set @math{{\{1, 3, 6, 10\}}}
by listing the elements in any order, our new representation allows only the
list @code{[1; 3; 6; 10]}.

One advantage of ordering shows up in @code{element_of_set}: In checking for
the presence of an item, we no longer have to scan the entire set.  If we reach
a set element that is larger than the item we are looking for, then we know
that the item is not in the set:

@example ocaml
let rec element_of_set x = function
  | [] -> false
  | first :: _ when x = first -> true
  | first :: _ when x < first -> false
  | _ :: rest -> element_of_set x rest
;;
@end example

@noindent
How many steps does this save?  In the worst case, the item we are looking for
may be the largest one in the set, so the number of steps is the same as for
the unordered representation.  On the other hand, if we search for items of
many different sizes we can expect that sometimes we will be able to stop
searching at a point near the beginning of the list and that other times we
will still need to examine most of the list.  On the average we should expect
to have to examine about half of the items in the set.  Thus, the average
number of steps required will be about @math{{n / 2}}.  This is still
@math{{\Theta(n)}} growth, but it does save us, on the average, a factor of 2
in number of steps over the previous implementation.

We obtain a more impressive speedup with @code{intersection_set}.  In the
unordered representation this operation required @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} steps,
because we performed a complete scan of @code{set2} for each element of
@code{set1}.  But with the ordered representation, we can use a more clever
method.  Begin by comparing the initial elements, @code{x1} and @code{x2}, of
the two sets.  If @code{x1} equals @code{x2}, then that gives an element of the
intersection, and the rest of the intersection is the intersection of the
rests of the two sets.  Suppose, however, that @code{x1} is less than
@code{x2}.  Since @code{x2} is the smallest element in @code{set2}, we can
immediately conclude that @code{x1} cannot appear anywhere in @code{set2} and
hence is not in the intersection.  Hence, the intersection is equal to the
intersection of @code{set2} with the rest of @code{set1}.  Similarly, if
@code{x2} is less than @code{x1}, then the intersection is given by the
intersection of @code{set1} with the rest of @code{set2}.  Here is the
procedure:

@example ocaml
let rec intersection_set set1 set2 =
  match (set1, set2) with
  | [], _ | _, [] -> []
  | first1 :: rest1, first2 :: rest2 ->
    if first1 = first2
    then first1 :: intersection_set rest1 rest2
    else if first1 < first2
    then intersection_set rest1 set2
    else intersection_set set1 rest2
;;
@end example

@noindent
To estimate the number of steps required by this process, observe that at each
step we reduce the intersection problem to computing intersections of smaller
sets---removing the first element from @code{set1} or @code{set2} or both.
Thus, the number of steps required is at most the sum of the sizes of
@code{set1} and @code{set2}, rather than the product of the sizes as with the
unordered representation.  This is @math{{\Theta(n)}} growth rather than
@math{{\Theta(n^2)}}---a considerable speedup, even for sets of moderate size.

@quotation
